.\" Man page for csv
.TH CSV 1 "November 2025" "csv 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
csv \- Unix-style commands for CSV files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B csv head
.RB [ \-\fInumber\fP ]
.RB [ \-a ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B csv tail
.RB [ \-\fInumber\fP ]
.RB [ \-a ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B csv wc
.RB [ \-lwc ]
.RB [ \-a ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B csv cut
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]
.I field
.RI [ field ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B csv
is a collection of Unix-style utilities for working with CSV files on a
row basis rather than a line basis. This properly handles CSV files with
quoted fields that may contain newlines or commas.
.PP
CSV files may optionally begin with comment lines starting with
.BR # .
These comment lines are preserved and output along with the header when
the
.B \-a
option is used.
.SS Commands
.TP
.B head
Display the first
.I n
data rows of a CSV file (default: 10).
.TP
.B tail
Display the last
.I n
data rows of a CSV file (default: 10).
.TP
.B wc
Count and display the number of rows, columns, and bytes in a CSV file.
Similar to
.BR wc (1)
but operates on CSV structure.
.TP
.B cut
Select and display specific fields (columns) from a CSV file by field name.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Options for head and tail
.TP
.BI \- number
Specify the number of rows to display. Default is 10.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-add\-header
Include the header row in output. If the CSV file begins with comment
lines (starting with #), these are also included.
.SS Options for wc
.TP
.B \-l
Display only the row count.
.TP
.B \-w
Display only the column count.
.TP
.B \-c
Display only the byte count.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-add\-header
Include the header row in the row count. By default, only data rows are
counted (the header is excluded).
.PP
Options may be combined (e.g.,
.BR \-lw ).
If no options are specified, all three counts are displayed.
.SS Options for cut
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-add\-header
Include the header row in output. If the CSV file begins with comment
lines (starting with #), these are also included.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Specify the input file. Use
.B \-
to explicitly read from standard input. If not specified, reads from
standard input.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
The CSV file to process. For
.BR head ,
.BR tail ,
and
.BR wc ,
if no file is specified, standard input is read. Use
.B \-
to explicitly read from standard input.
.TP
.I field
For the
.B cut
command, one or more field names (column headers) to select from the CSV file.
Fields are output in the order specified.
.SH STDIN
When no file operand is specified (or when
.B \-
is given), the commands read from standard input. This allows
.B csv
commands to be used in pipelines.
.SH STDOUT
Output is written to standard output in CSV format. The
.B wc
command outputs counts in a format similar to
.BR wc (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred, such as an unknown field name for
.BR cut ,
an invalid option, or a file that could not be read.
.SH EXAMPLES
Display the first 5 data rows of a CSV file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
csv head \-5 data.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Display the first 5 rows with header and comments:
.PP
.RS
.nf
csv head \-5 \-a data.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Display the last 3 rows with header:
.PP
.RS
.nf
csv tail \-3 \-a data.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Count rows, columns, and bytes (excluding header from row count):
.PP
.RS
.nf
csv wc data.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Count rows including header:
.PP
.RS
.nf
csv wc \-a data.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Count only data rows:
.PP
.RS
.nf
csv wc \-l data.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Select specific columns by name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
csv cut \-f data.csv name email phone
.fi
.RE
.PP
Select columns with header included:
.PP
.RS
.nf
csv cut \-a \-f data.csv name email
.fi
.RE
.PP
Select columns from standard input:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cat data.csv | csv cut name email
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read from standard input:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cat data.csv | csv head \-1 \-a
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipeline example \(em select columns then show first 5 rows:
.PP
.RS
.nf
csv cut \-a \-f data.csv name city | csv head \-5 \-a
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipeline example \(em filter then select columns:
.PP
.RS
.nf
csv head \-10 \-a data.csv | csv cut \-a name city
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
.SS Row vs Line Processing
Unlike standard Unix utilities that operate on lines,
.B csv
operates on CSV rows. A single CSV row may span multiple lines if it
contains quoted fields with embedded newlines. This tool correctly
handles such cases.
.SS Header Handling
The first non-comment row of a CSV file is treated as the header row.
By default,
.B head
and
.B tail
output only data rows (excluding the header). Use
.B \-a
to include the header in output.
.SS Comment Lines
Lines starting with
.B #
at the very beginning of a CSV file are treated as comment lines.
These are preserved when using the
.B \-a
option and are considered part of the header for output purposes.
Comment lines are not counted as rows by
.BR wc .
.SS Column Count
The
.B wc
command reports the number of columns based on the header row (first
non-comment row). All rows are assumed to have the same number of columns.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR head (1),
.BR tail (1),
.BR wc (1),
.BR cut (1)
.SH BUGS
Report bugs to the maintainer.
.SH AUTHOR
Written for CSV file processing tasks.
